# FSD Architecture Rules

This project follows Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) methodology.

## Layer Hierarchy (strict top-down imports only)
app → pages → widgets → features → entities → shared

A layer can ONLY import from layers below it. Never sideways, never upward.

## Slice Structure
Each slice follows the segment convention:
- ui/       — React components
- model/    — state, stores, business logic (zustand/redux/effector)
- api/      — API requests, data fetching
- lib/      — helpers, utilities specific to the slice
- config/   — constants, configs
- types/    — TypeScript types

## Public API Rule
Every slice MUST expose functionality via index.ts (public API).
Never import from deep paths across slices/layers like `features/auth/ui/LoginForm/LoginForm.tsx`.
Use slice root imports like `@/features/auth`.
Inside the same slice, use relative imports between segments (`../model`, `../lib`) to keep boundaries explicit.

## Naming
- Layers: lowercase plural (features, entities, widgets)
- Slices: lowercase kebab-case or camelCase (auth, user-profile)
- Components: PascalCase

## Cross-imports between slices of the same layer are FORBIDDEN
If feature-A needs feature-B, lift the shared logic to entities or shared.

## Connectors and adapters
- Connector = HTTP/WS call function, keep it in slice `api/` segment.
- Adapter = mapping `api dto -> domain model`, keep it in `entities/*/model` or `entities/*/lib`.
- UI must not consume raw DTO from backend directly.

## Shared UI atomic levels
- `shared/ui/atoms` - basic primitive building blocks
- `shared/ui/molecules` - composed controls
- `shared/ui/organisms` - larger reusable blocks
- `shared/ui/templates` - page skeletons without business logic